class:: Limcycle
summary:: Relaxation oscillator with stochastic forcing
related:: Classes/Oscnet, Classes/Fracflight
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic

description::

A self-oscillating nonlinear ODE integrated per sample with RK4, in nondimensional
time, so code::mu::, code::force:: and code::noise:: are in natural units while
code::freq:: sets the natural frequency.

list::
## code::shape:: morphs the nonlinear damping from position-based (0, sine to
relaxation buzz) to velocity-based (1, a different asymmetry).
## code::well:: morphs the restoring force from a single well (0) to a double well
(1), where the rest point at 0 becomes unstable and the state can flip between basins.
## code::feedback:: adds a slow feedback state, making the flow three-dimensional.
That is what admits chaos: at code::feedback = 0:: the flow is two-dimensional and can
only settle into a limit cycle. Chaos also needs code::force > 0:: (or code::noise::);
the autonomous system stays periodic. It arrives in windows rather than everywhere, so
sweep code::feedback:: and code::fbRate:: to find them.
## code::noise:: injects heavy-tailed (Lévy) kicks shaped by code::tail::, so the cycle
is jostled by rare large impulses.
::

Two outputs: code::[position x, velocity x']::. Pitch tracks code::freq:: exactly only
near code::mu = 0::; the period stretches as code::mu:: and code::well:: rise. Sub-steps
are added automatically beyond code::oversample:: when code::freq:: or code::mu:: would
otherwise destabilise the integrator.

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: freq
Natural frequency (Hz). Default 110.
argument:: mu
Nonlinearity / damping, 0–50. ~0 sine, higher = relaxation. Default 1.
argument:: shape
Damping morph: 0 position-based (1-x^2), 1 velocity-based (1-x'^2). Default 0.
argument:: well
Potential morph: 0 single well, 1 double well. Default 0.
argument:: force
Amplitude of the sinusoidal forcing term, natural units (roughly 0–10). Default 0.
argument:: forceFreq
Frequency of the forcing term (Hz). Default 55.
argument:: feedback
Slow-feedback strength, 0–20. Above 0 the flow is 3D and can be chaotic. Default 0.
argument:: fbRate
Relaxation rate of the feedback state, 0.001–4. Low is slow. Default 0.1.
argument:: noise
Amount of heavy-tailed stochastic forcing. Default 0.
argument:: tail
Lévy stability index for the noise, 0.5–2. 2 = Gaussian, lower = heavier tails. Default 2.
argument:: oversample
Integration sub-steps per sample, 1–32. Default 4.
argument:: mul
argument:: add

examples::
code::
{ Limcycle.ar(110, MouseX.kr(0.05, 12), oversample: 16)[0] * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(110, 6, MouseX.kr(0, 1), oversample: 16)[0] * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(80, 6, 0, 1, 2, 120, MouseX.kr(0, 4), 0.05, oversample: 16)[0] * 0.25 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(110, 0.05, 0, 1, 8, 110, 3, MouseX.kr(0.01, 0.3), oversample: 16)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(80, 5, 0, 0, MouseX.kr(0, 10), 120, oversample: 12)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(110, 3, 0, 0.2, 0, 55, 0, 0.1, MouseX.kr(0, 1.5), MouseY.kr(2, 0.6), 16)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

{ Limcycle.ar(MouseX.kr(40, 400, 1), 8, 0.5, 1, 2.5, 90, 1.5, 0.03, 0.2, 1.2, 16)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

{ var l = Limcycle.ar(90, MouseY.kr(1, 10), 0, 1, 3, 137, 2, 0.04, oversample: 16);
  ((l[0] * 0.25) + (l[1] * 0.1)) ! 2 }.play;

{ var l = Limcycle.ar(60, 7, 0, 1, 2, 93, 2.5, 0.03, oversample: 16);
  SinOsc.ar(300 + (l[0] * 600)) * l[1].abs * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;
::
